EXT. DRIVE-IN THEATER - CONCESSION ROOF - NIGHT

A flat tar roof above the snack bar. Beyond the low parapet, rows of parked cars face a giant screen flickering with a western none of them are watching. Moths swarm the projector beam.

DEWEY HALLORAN, seventeen, lean, in a rented bow tie, sits cross-legged with a flashlight in his teeth. LONNIE VASQUEZ, seventeen, stocky, in a usher's jacket two sizes too small, sits beside him eating popcorn from a paper tub. FRANKIE DUBOIS, sixteen, wiry, with grease under his nails, stands at the edge and looks down.

DEWEY
Everybody got their cut of the money?

LONNIE
I got mine. I got it in my sock.

FRANKIE
Why's it in your sock?

LONNIE
Because that's where my mother never looks.

DEWEY
Fine. Frankie, you're sure about the key?

FRANKIE
I made it myself. It'll open any projection booth in the county.

LONNIE
The county? Frankie, we've got one booth.

FRANKIE
It's a figure of speech.

DEWEY
It's a good key, Lonnie. Leave him alone.

LONNIE
I'm only saying.

FRANKIE
You're always only saying.

DEWEY
All right, listen. Tonight we show them the thing we made. Everybody's out there, the whole school, my father, Mr. Paget. After the intermission cartoon, it plays. Three minutes. That's all.

LONNIE
My mother will see it.

DEWEY
Your mother will love it.

LONNIE
She's going to see me in it, Dewey. Me, with the cape.

FRANKIE
You look great in the cape.

LONNIE
I look like a tablecloth.

DEWEY
You look like a hero.

LONNIE
A tablecloth hero.

Lonnie stuffs a fistful of popcorn into his mouth. Dewey takes the flashlight from his teeth and clicks it off.

FRANKIE
What if it doesn't work?

DEWEY
It'll work.

FRANKIE
What if the reel jams?

LONNIE
What if it plays backward?

DEWEY
Then it'll be a different movie.

FRANKIE
You always say that.

DEWEY
Because it's always true.

Below them, the western reaches a gunfight. A hundred car horns honk in approval, and the roof hums with it.

LONNIE
That's not even a good one.

FRANKIE
Mine's better.

DEWEY
Ours is better.

LONNIE
Ours.

Frankie turns from the edge and sits between them. The three of them look out over the lot. Somewhere a screen door bangs.

FRANKIE
What do we do after?

DEWEY
After what?

FRANKIE
After they see it.

DEWEY
We go home.

LONNIE
We go home and pretend we weren't here.

FRANKIE
And then?

DEWEY
Then we make another one.

LONNIE
(through popcorn)
A longer one.

FRANKIE
With real stunts.

DEWEY
With no capes.

LONNIE
No, no, the cape stays.

The western ends. The screen goes white, then black. A tinny voice from the loudspeakers announces the intermission. A dancing hot dog appears on screen, singing.

Dewey stands. Lonnie stands. Frankie is already on his feet.

DEWEY (CONT'D)
Frankie. The booth.

FRANKIE
Going.

LONNIE
I'm coming with you.

DEWEY
Both of you, go. I'll watch the lot.

FRANKIE
Don't let anybody leave.

DEWEY
They won't.

Frankie vaults over the roof's far edge to the ladder. Lonnie is on his heels. Dewey stays at the parapet, shoulders squared, watching a hundred cars and the dancing hot dog. He lifts the flashlight and holds it like a torch.

DEWEY (CONT'D)
(softly)
Now.

The loudspeakers crackle. The screen flickers and goes dark. For one long second the whole lot is silent, a hundred windshields reflecting nothing.

Then a projector lamp flares in the booth and a ragged, hand-cut image leaps up on the screen: a boy in a tablecloth cape, running.

Dewey grins. Down in the lot, a horn honks. Then another. Then all of them.
